{
  "schemaVersion": 3,
  "id": "middle-distance",
  "name": "Middle Distance",
  "tagline": "The harmony moves inside; the top stays still.",
  "description": "A held four-part chord whose two inner parts step to new notes every few minutes, each change a slow dissolve, while the lowest and highest notes never move. Deep Work's pulse, bass and ticks underneath; open-fifth dub chords and the acid line come and go. No scenes: nothing paints a picture.",
  "use": {
    "name": "Design",
    "for": "Thinking in pictures: design, diagrams, architecture, systems.",
    "why": "Thinking in images runs on a different code from words, and the evidence divides the same way. Speech disrupts rote verbal rehearsal but not imagery, and moving visual noise does the reverse. Changing sound sequences that disrupt verbal order memory did not disrupt spatial memory in recent replications. So here the music may move, slowly, inside the chord, one part at a time and as a dissolve, while nothing jumps out: a sudden event disrupts any kind of work. The top note never moves, because pitch changes on top are the ones the ear tracks most, and the beat sits in the bass, where timing is heard best. No scene sounds: whether imagery-rich sound competes with visual thinking is untested, so pictures stay out as a precaution."
  },
